## Unit 4a: Linked Lists

### Implementing Basic Operations

#### Push:

```cpp
void List::push_front(int value) {
    Node* newNode = new Node(value);
    if (head == nullptr) {
        head = newNode;
        tail = newNode;
    } else {
        newNode->next = head;
        head = newNode;
    }
}
```

#### Pop:

```cpp
int List::pop_front() {
    if (head == nullptr) {
        throw std::runtime_error("List is empty");
    }

    int value = head->value;
    Node* temp = head;
    head = head->next;
    delete temp;
    if (head == nullptr) {
        tail = nullptr;
    }

    return value;
}
```

### Efficiency and Variations

- **With Tail Pointer:** Provides quick access to the last element but requires extra memory.
- **Without Tail Pointer:** Slower access times to the end but uses less memory.

### Pros and Cons Compared to Arrays

#### Pros of Linked Lists:
- Dynamic size
- Insertion and deletion at any position is efficient
- Memory allocation is done on demand

#### Cons of Linked Lists:
- Sequential access is slow (O(n))
- Requires additional memory overhead

## Unit 4b: `vector<T>` and `deque<T>` Basic Operations

### `vector<T>` Operations

#### Push:

```cpp
void MyVector::push_back(T value) {
    resize(size() + 1);
    ptr[size() - 1] = value;
}
```

#### Pop:

```cpp
T MyVector::pop_back() {
    T value = ptr[size() - 1];
    resize(size() - 1);
    return value;
}
```

### Efficiency

- `vector<T>` has O(1) amortized time complexity for push and pop operations.

## Unit 4c: Doubly Linked Lists and `deque<T>` Implementation

### Doubly Linked List Operations

#### Push:

```cpp
void DoublyLinkedList::push_front(int value) {
    Node* newNode = new Node(value);
    if (head == nullptr) {
        head = newNode;
        tail = newNode;
    } else {
        newNode->next = head;
        head->prev = newNode;
        head = newNode;
    }
}
```

#### Efficiency

- Doubly linked list provides efficient insertion and deletion at both ends (O(1)).

### `deque<T>` Operations

#### Push:

```cpp
void MyDeque::push_front(T value) {
    if (front == nullptr) {
        front = new Node(value);
        back = front;
    } else {
        Node* newNode = new Node(value);
        newNode->next = front;
        front->prev = newNode;
        front = newNode;
    }
}
```

#### Efficiency

- `deque<T>` supports fast access, insertion, and deletion at both ends (O(1)).

## Unit 4d: Multidimensional Arrays

### Memory Layout

#### 2D Array:
```cpp
int arr[3][4] = {0};
```
The memory layout is as follows:
```
arr[0][0] arr[0][1] arr[0][2] arr[0][3]
arr[1][0] arr[1][1] arr[1][2] arr[1][3]
arr[2][0] arr[2][1] arr[2][2] arr[2][3]
```

### Passing to Functions

#### Example:
```cpp
void printMatrix(int matrix[][4], int rows, int cols) {
    for (int i = 0; i < rows; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < cols; ++j) {
            cout << matrix[i][j] << " ";
        }
        cout << endl;
    }
}
```

## Unit 4e: References and Passing by Reference

### Syntax and Use

#### Declaration:
```cpp
void swap(int& x, int& y) {
    int temp = x;
    x = y;
    y = temp;
}
```

#### Passing by Reference:

```cpp
void modifyArray(int (&arr)[3]) {
    arr[0] = 10;
}
```

### Advantages of Passing by Reference

- Avoids copying large data structures
- Faster execution due to reduced memory usage

### const References

#### Usage:
```cpp
void printArray(const int (&arr)[3]) {
    for (const int& elem : arr) {
        cout << elem << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
}
```

## Unit 4f: File I/O with `ifstream` and `ofstream`

### Opening Files

#### Syntax:
```cpp
std::ifstream fileStream("filename.txt");
std::ofstream fileStream("filename.txt");
```

### Reading and Writing Files

#### Reading:

```cpp
int value;
fileStream >> value;
```

#### Writing:

```cpp
fileStream << "Hello, World!";
```

### Checking for Failure

#### Function Syntax:
```cpp
bool fileFailed = !fileStream.eof();
```

#### Usage:
```cpp
if (!fileFailed) {
    // Read or write successful
} else {
    // Handle error
}
```

This comprehensive guide should provide you with a solid understanding and practical application of C++ concepts covered in Units 3a through 4f.

